      "dek": "Each trajectory was handed a different amount of speed before launch, depending on which planets it was pointed at.",
      "affiliateBox": null,
      "content": "<p><h2>The manifest and the scoreboard disagree</h2></p><p>The manifest says Voyager 2 flew first. The scoreboard says Voyager 1 is far ahead of it. The later probe reached the distance light covers in a day nearly a decade before the earlier one. That is what the report behind this piece says. Nothing in a launch date predicts that outcome.</p><p>The gap was designed in, long before anyone measured it. Each spacecraft was given an amount of energy relative to the Sun, and the amount was set by what its mission was asked to see. Voyager 1 was aimed to be quick. Voyager 2 was aimed to arrive at more places. The gap at a light-day is what those two aims do.</p><h2>The yardstick inside the claim</h2><p>Start with the yardstick, since it is doing quiet work. A light-day is how far light travels in twenty-four hours, which is a distance and not a duration. Converting it into astronomical units is arithmetic on definitions rather than a measurement, and the answer is a little over 170 of them. So the crossing point both probes had to reach sits roughly 170 times the Earth-Sun line out.</p><p>Two things follow. The unit is the same for both spacecraft at the same instant. A comparison of total distance traveled cannot offer that, since the two left Earth on different days. And the distance is far enough out to be interesting. Light-time of that length means every figure about either probe reaches us already stale.</p><h2>The physics is a subtraction</h2><p>A gravity assist moves energy from a planet in orbit to a spacecraft passing it. The planet is going around the Sun fast, and the flyby geometry decides how much of that motion ends up in the probe. The direction matters more than the closeness. A pass arranged to add speed leaves with more; the same planet, arranged differently, hands over less and points the craft somewhere else.</p><p>Nothing out there is braking.</p><p>So the difference between the two probes is fixed at the last assist and then accumulates. A probe with more residual speed covers more ground per year than one with less, and it does so in a straight line, without refueling. That is why a late start can still buy a lead measured in years. It follows that the gap in arrival time at a distance grows in proportion to that distance. A light-day costs a decade, and twice as far out costs roughly twice as much time, provided neither speed changes much. The gap is a rate multiplied by a distance, and nothing more mysterious.</p><h2>Two trajectories, two different products</h2><p>The mission record frames the pair as two different jobs. One Voyager was put on the quick path to Saturn and its largest moon. The other was put on the slower path that kept the far planets reachable, and the flight team has said so publicly since the program began. That explains why the distance table surprises anyone reading only the launch dates. The two spacecraft were never sold as the same product.</p><p>Two variables are usually confused here. The first is the launch vehicle, and a booster cannot explain a decade. Whatever the upper stage did, it did in the first hours; the difference in question persists for years. The second is the boost from each planetary flyby, which is a design choice made before launch by people computing encounter geometry. The scarce resource in this program was encounter geometry. The schedule bent around it, which is why the two launch dates sit close together and tell you nothing.</p><h2>The strongest objection is that this was never a race</h2><p>Grant the best version of the case against the framing. The program was designed to visit planets, not to set distance records, and the probe that did more visiting finished second on a leaderboard nobody scored. On that account, the decade gap is trivia: an artifact of picking one number out of many and calling it a result.</p><p>Concede all of that, and then ask the mechanical question. A fairer ranking of these two missions would count encounters, and Voyager 2 would win it. The gap itself has a cause that the ranking does not touch: the energy each trajectory was handed before either probe left the ground. A reader can refuse the scoreboard and still want to know why it looks like that.</p><h2>Distance and boundary are different questions</h2><p>Distance and boundary are different questions, and the public conversation keeps merging them. A probe reaching a light-day is a statement about geometry and speed. A probe leaving the heliosphere is a statement about where the local boundary happened to be. There is no reason to expect that boundary to sit at one radius in every direction. It is one flowing medium pressing against another, and interfaces like that are lopsided.</p><p>The same caution applies to the unit itself. Nobody planned a crossing at one light-day; the figure exists so readers can compare two paths at one instant. What each probe was actually aimed at was a planet, and the distance column is a byproduct of the flybys that made the aiming necessary.</p><h2>What this piece could not check</h2><p>One secondary source sits behind this piece, and it could not be re-derived here. Four figures would settle what remains open. They are the residual speed of each probe against the Sun and the current distance of each from it. The other two worth having are the date each crossed one light-day out and the angle of each path to the planetary plane. None of those four is asserted above, and none changes the mechanism.</p><p>A limit worth stating: the account above is a mechanism, and mechanisms are not measurements. The mission-design claim, that a slower trajectory is what kept the far planets reachable, should be read against program documentation rather than a news summary. The two probes' residual speeds could be closer than a decade at a light-day suggests. In that case the gap owes less to encounter geometry than the account above allows. It would not put the launch date back in charge.</p><h2>How to check who is ahead</h2><p>The distance figures NASA publishes are easy to read badly. Take the column that gives distance from the Sun and note the date stamped on the page. Ignore any figure describing distance traveled, because the two probes left on different days. A light-day is not on that table, so convert it once and keep it: a little over 170 astronomical units.</p><p>Open the table, take the number, then subtract the light-time. At one light-day out, the value on the page describes where the probe was a full day earlier. The reply from it arrives a day later again. The page carries its timestamp, which is the only reason that arithmetic is possible. Anyone reading the figure as a present-tense fact is one day behind.</p><p>Now divide the light-day distance by each probe's speed and compare the two answers. If they differ by roughly the reported number of years, the trajectory explanation holds and nobody was unlucky. If they differ by much more or much less, something in the mechanism above is doing less work than it claims.</p>",
