      "dek": "A report from southern China reads an unusual crown as company for Homo erectus. What the specimen can prove and what the headline implies are different claims.",
      "affiliateBox": null,
      "content": "<h2>The verb in the headline is the story</h2><p>A report that reached the wire on 18 November describes one unusual tooth from southern China. The reading attached to it is that Homo erectus shared that region with another human species, in the same place and at the same time. The sentence is careful, and the careful word is suggests. The second species goes unnamed.</p><p>This column's claim is narrow. A single tooth can prove that one body plan existed somewhere. It cannot prove that two human species occupied the same place at the same time. The second claim is the interesting one, and a tooth is the wrong instrument for it, whatever the specimen turns out to look like.</p><h2>Why teeth run this argument</h2><p>Paleoanthropology leans on teeth because teeth are what survives. Jaw bone is thin and porous. It is usually gone. Enamel keeps the shape of a crown long after the rest of a skeleton is a mineral smear. That is why regional debates in East Asia are so often settled on dental evidence.</p><p>Teeth are the field's hard currency, and they are cheap to compare. A molar fits in calipers and on a spreadsheet. Sample sizes that other bones never reach are routine, which lets an outlier be measured against a distribution rather than a guess. That is a real advantage, and it is where the argument goes wrong.</p><h2>What one molar can carry</h2><p>A well-described tooth does hold information. Crown outline, ridge pattern, enamel thickness, root slope and the wear facet that chewing cut into the surface are heritable in broad strokes. Those traits separate recognized species well enough that systematicians have spent careers on them. An outlier sitting outside every comparative sample is a real signal.</p><p>The strongest honest version of this report is narrow but not trivial. One tooth exists whose shape is hard to place inside the spread of the recognized form from that region. That claim needs no second specimen. It needs a comparative sample and a published figure, and readers should expect both before the sentence is accepted.</p><h2>Coexistence is a different order of claim</h2><p>Difference in shape and difference in company are different claims. Shape says that two body plans existed somewhere, at some point. Sympatry says they were within reach of one another, in overlapping layers, inside the error bars of the dating used. The headline states the second claim, and a crown delivers only the first.</p><p>One tooth is one specimen.</p><p>To turn one specimen into coexistence, two things besides the tooth have to line up. There is its position in the sediment, which must be secure rather than inferred. There is an age for that layer, and the age must overlap the material assigned to the recognized species. Nothing about enamel supplies either one. A crown records a chewing life, and that is a different record from the ground around it.</p><h2>The strongest case against the reading</h2><p>Give the skeptics the best version. A single outlier in a small dental sample is a common thing, and it usually disappears when more specimens arrive. Wear flattens crowns. A mineral that pressed on the crown during burial can fill a groove and change the profile. Developmental irregularity produces odd teeth in every population ever counted, human beings included.</p><p>Then there is the region. Southern China has a long record of hominin remains whose features sit awkwardly between the textbook forms, and the discipline has argued about them for generations. A reader who already distrusts tidy species boundaries will find this report comforting rather than destabilizing. The discipline has named a species from one molar and quietly reversed the name in a footnote.</p><h2>What this column has not seen</h2><p>The inputs here are a headline, a dated summary and nothing else. The specimen figures are not in view, and neither is the comparative sample. The stratigraphic description and the dating method are equally absent, and so is any age. So the specific measurements that would let anyone check this claim are unreported, and the number of specimens described is unknown.</p><p>There is a further limit that no amount of access lifts. A tooth in a cave deposit may have entered the sediment at a different moment from the bones beside it. Burrowing leaves nothing a crown would record. Stratigraphy has to be argued, not assumed. If the layer was never clean, no laboratory result makes it clean.</p><h2>What coexistence would explain</h2><p>There is a reason the field wants this claim, beyond tidiness. If two hominin lineages occupied one region at one time, the archaeological record of that region stops being a safe proxy for biology. A tool type becomes something a lineage learned or copied, and nobody can say which from a tooth. That is a large consequence, and it is why the standard applied to one specimen is high.</p><p>The counterweight is simple. Speculation is easy; a tooth with secure provenance is a season of digging. Every region that produced a mixed assemblage eventually produced a revision, and several produced two. Coexistence claims have a habit of resolving into one wide species, which is a dull sentence and frequently the correct one.</p><h2>What would settle it</h2><p>Two findings would end this argument in either direction. A second tooth of the same unusual shape, from the same layer, removes the individual variation explanation almost entirely. A secure age for that layer, taken on the sediment rather than on the fossil, decides whether the two forms were ever together. Both are obtainable.</p><p>Imaging helps without settling anything. Internal structure scanned in detail and enamel thickness measured across the crown behave differently from surface shape. They can place an odd tooth closer to a recognized species or push it further from it, depending on the trait. Either result describes the specimen, and neither one dates the deposit.</p><h2>How to read the paper when it lands</h2><p>Ignore the adjectives in whatever summary reaches you and go straight to the description, where four lines answer most of the question. Which teeth were measured against which comparative sample, and how many specimens are in each group. Whether the odd tooth came from an excavated layer or from loose material gathered at the surface. What the dating was run on, and by which method. Whether the authors write that the specimen belongs to another species, or only resembles one.</p><p>If those answers hold, the coexistence claim deserves the attention it is getting, and this column has been too cautious. If the layer is unsecured, what exists is an odd tooth from a region that produces odd teeth, and the second species stays unnamed. That check is quick once the paper is public, and it is worth running before the claim becomes common sense.</p>",
